      "text": "Chinese phones have long been purchased by low-income consumers worldwide thanks to their affordability; however, if rising labor and component costs drive up their prices, they will likely cease to sell. While ordinary consumers have traditionally bought these phones for around $70.73, prices have recently been on the rise. The issue is that their primary appeal was their low cost; if the price point matches that of products from countries with greater reliability, there is no longer any compelling reason to choose the Chinese option. It is human nature to prefer products from more reliable nations when prices are comparable, and since these Chinese products rely on government subsidies to sell, they struggle to compete without that financial support.",
